<title>NEPAL: Nepal increases minimum wage.</title>
<headline>Nepal increases minimum wage.</headline>
<dateline>KATHMANDU 1997-08-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>Nepal announced on Monday it will raise the minimum wage for some 350,000 workers by up to 30 percent.</p>
<p>The Labour Ministry said in a statement in the capital Kathmandu that the floor on monthly wages for unskilled workers will be raised by 300 rupees ($5.3) to 1,300 from September 17.</p>
<p>The minimum wage for three other categories of labourers will also be increased by 300 rupees to 1,350 for semi-skilled workers, 1,460 for skilled workers and 1,650 for highly skilled workers.</p>
<p>The government established the new minimum wages in talks with employers.</p>
<p>Nepal is one of the 10 poorest countries with per capita gross national product of $200 in 1995, according to the World Bank. About 80 percent of the workforce in the country of 21.5 million people is employed in agriculture. ($1 = 56.9 rupees)</p>
</text>
